WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.369
Lesson three point three.

2
00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:04.081
How the model uses its loss to get better,

3
00:00:04.081 --> 00:00:05.091
one step at a time.

4
00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:10.730
Lesson three point one built a weighted

5
00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:11.103
sum, z.

6
00:00:11.352 --> 00:00:13.009
Lesson three point two turned it into a

7
00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.790
probability with the sigmoid, and scored

8
00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.489
it with binary cross-entropy, the loss.

9
00:00:16.738 --> 00:00:18.809
Here's a made-up case: one input, x equals

10
00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:20.798
two, one weight, w equals a half, and the

11
00:00:20.798 --> 00:00:22.123
answer is a hit, y equals one.

12
00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:24.402
z is one, and sigma, the sigmoid, makes it

13
00:00:24.402 --> 00:00:26.266
zero point seven three one, so the loss,

14
00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:28.130
minus the log of that, is zero point three

15
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:28.545
one three.

16
00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:31.304
Slide the weight and the loss changes:

17
00:00:31.304 --> 00:00:33.565
this curve is the loss for every value of

18
00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:33.673
w.

19
00:00:33.996 --> 00:00:34.912
We sit at a half.

20
00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:37.368
The line touching the curve there has a

21
00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:39.732
slope of minus zero point five four: nudge

22
00:00:39.732 --> 00:00:41.799
w up a little, and the loss drops about

23
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:42.440
half as much.

24
00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:44.163
That slope is the derivative.

25
00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:46.920
Its sign says which way is downhill: here,

26
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:47.462
a bigger w.

27
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:48.939
Its size says how steep.

28
00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:51.155
Far to the right, where the model is

29
00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:53.173
already sure, the curve is nearly flat.

30
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.951
Now the same slope by hand.

31
00:00:56.324 --> 00:00:59.001
The curly d means a slope for one quantity

32
00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:00.994
with everything else held still.

33
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:04.169
L depends on z only through p, so multiply

34
00:01:04.169 --> 00:01:07.157
two slopes: L against p, and p against z.

35
00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.270
For y equals one the loss is minus log p,

36
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.262
whose slope is minus one over p.

37
00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:15.188
The sigmoid's slope is p times one minus

38
00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:15.312
p.

39
00:01:15.686 --> 00:01:18.487
Multiply, the p cancels, and what's left

40
00:01:18.487 --> 00:01:20.791
is p minus one, which is p minus y.

41
00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:24.090
With y equals zero the same steps give p:

42
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.086
p minus y again.

43
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.658
z is w times x, so one more link

44
00:01:27.658 --> 00:01:29.945
multiplies by x: the error times the

45
00:01:29.945 --> 00:01:30.271
input.

46
00:01:30.598 --> 00:01:32.776
Zero point seven three one minus one,

47
00:01:32.776 --> 00:01:35.172
times two: minus zero point five three

48
00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:37.568
eight, the slope of the line on the curve.

49
00:01:37.895 --> 00:01:40.127
So a confident right answer barely moves

50
00:01:40.127 --> 00:01:42.469
the weight, and an input of zero doesn't

51
00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:43.285
move it at all.

52
00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:46.002
The gradient points uphill, so step the

53
00:01:46.002 --> 00:01:48.342
other way: w minus eta times the slope.

54
00:01:48.661 --> 00:01:49.991
Eta is the learning rate.

55
00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.703
With eta at zero point one, w goes to zero

56
00:01:52.703 --> 00:01:54.989
point five five four, and the loss falls

57
00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:56.532
to zero point two eight five.

58
00:01:56.851 --> 00:01:59.403
That's gradient descent: find the slope,

59
00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:00.680
step downhill, repeat.

60
00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.453
A real model has a slope for every weight,

61
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:08.731
and the list of them all is the gradient,

62
00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:10.750
written nabla L, with an upside-down

63
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:11.216
triangle.

64
00:02:11.526 --> 00:02:13.597
Our transformer drummer has two hundred

65
00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:15.771
and twenty-six thousand eight hundred and

66
00:02:15.771 --> 00:02:17.790
fifty-seven parameters, so that many

67
00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:18.153
slopes.

68
00:02:18.556 --> 00:02:20.563
The forward pass runs left to right:

69
00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:22.809
guitar features in, through the layers, to

70
00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.100
nine logits, to the loss.

71
00:02:24.387 --> 00:02:25.486
The gradient runs back.

72
00:02:25.773 --> 00:02:27.828
The loss hands each logit its p minus y,

73
00:02:27.828 --> 00:02:29.739
and each layer going back multiplies in

74
00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:31.986
its own slope: the chain rule, one link

75
00:02:31.986 --> 00:02:33.706
per layer, just as we did by hand.

76
00:02:33.993 --> 00:02:35.857
Doing that from the loss back to every

77
00:02:35.857 --> 00:02:37.099
weight is backpropagation.

78
00:02:37.531 --> 00:02:40.224
In our training loss, every real slot and

79
00:02:40.224 --> 00:02:42.673
every drum hands its logit p minus y,

80
00:02:42.673 --> 00:02:44.264
averaged over all of them.

81
00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:47.602
Here's the training step in our code.

82
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.426
Clear the last step's gradients.

83
00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:50.819
Then loss dot backward.

84
00:02:51.107 --> 00:02:53.027
PyTorch recorded every operation of the

85
00:02:53.027 --> 00:02:55.139
forward pass, and backward runs the chain

86
00:02:55.139 --> 00:02:57.203
rule back through that record: that's

87
00:02:57.203 --> 00:02:57.635
autograd.

88
00:02:57.923 --> 00:03:00.180
The next line caps the gradient's length:

89
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:00.852
chapter seven.

90
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.204
And opt dot step changes the weights:

91
00:03:03.204 --> 00:03:03.828
chapter five.

92
00:03:07.333 --> 00:03:08.457
How big should eta be?

93
00:03:08.764 --> 00:03:10.910
Take a made-up loss shaped like a bowl,

94
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.107
lowest at one and a half, and three runs

95
00:03:13.107 --> 00:03:14.487
that start at minus a half.

96
00:03:14.949 --> 00:03:17.154
Too small, and eight steps barely move it.

97
00:03:17.454 --> 00:03:19.609
About right, and it's near the bottom in

98
00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:19.909
three.

99
00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.214
Too big, and each step jumps past the

100
00:03:22.214 --> 00:03:24.569
bottom, higher than before: it diverges.

101
00:03:25.103 --> 00:03:27.446
Ours is zero point zero zero one, the

102
00:03:27.446 --> 00:03:29.906
trainer's default, in every run on file.

103
00:03:30.258 --> 00:03:33.011
With Adam, in chapter five, that's roughly

104
00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:35.061
how far each weight moves per step.

105
00:03:38.557 --> 00:03:40.375
Lesson two point eight put the songs in

106
00:03:40.375 --> 00:03:42.103
batches of thirty-two and walked each

107
00:03:42.103 --> 00:03:43.921
batch forward in chunks of five hundred

108
00:03:43.921 --> 00:03:44.694
and twelve slots.

109
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:46.831
Each block here is one chunk: up to

110
00:03:46.831 --> 00:03:48.422
sixteen thousand three hundred and

111
00:03:48.422 --> 00:03:49.241
eighty-four slots.

112
00:03:49.513 --> 00:03:51.423
Average the loss over a chunk, take one

113
00:03:51.423 --> 00:03:53.651
gradient, change the weights once: that's

114
00:03:53.651 --> 00:03:54.060
one step.

115
00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.151
One pass over every training song is an

116
00:03:56.151 --> 00:03:56.424
epoch.

117
00:03:56.925 --> 00:03:59.111
T one twenty-eight trained on fourteen

118
00:03:59.111 --> 00:04:01.579
thousand two hundred and fifty-six songs,

119
00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:03.990
and a batch of songs took about four point

120
00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.345
seven chunks, so four point seven steps.

121
00:04:06.681 --> 00:04:08.868
So an epoch was about two thousand one

122
00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:11.223
hundred steps, shifting a little as the

123
00:04:11.223 --> 00:04:12.737
shuffle regroups the songs.

124
00:04:13.073 --> 00:04:15.316
Twelve epochs made twenty-five thousand

125
00:04:15.316 --> 00:04:17.279
three hundred and fifty-nine steps.

126
00:04:17.615 --> 00:04:20.138
Rock took a thousand and sixteen an epoch,

127
00:04:20.138 --> 00:04:21.820
and Country ran twenty epochs.

128
00:04:22.157 --> 00:04:24.456
Each step's gradient is an estimate from

129
00:04:24.456 --> 00:04:26.418
one chunk: noisy, but cheap.

130
00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:31.432
Plain descent's step grows with the

131
00:04:31.432 --> 00:04:33.139
gradient, so some weights leap, some

132
00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.804
crawl, and one noisy step can shove a

133
00:04:34.804 --> 00:04:35.678
weight the wrong way.

134
00:04:35.928 --> 00:04:37.718
Adam, our optimiser, keeps two running

135
00:04:37.718 --> 00:04:38.800
averages for every weight.

136
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.173
The first, m, is momentum: nine tenths of

137
00:04:41.173 --> 00:04:43.088
the old m, a number called beta one, plus

138
00:04:43.088 --> 00:04:44.878
a tenth of the new gradient, roughly the

139
00:04:44.878 --> 00:04:46.627
last ten steps, which smooths the noise.

140
00:04:46.876 --> 00:04:48.708
For the toy's first step, m is a tenth of

141
00:04:48.708 --> 00:04:50.124
minus zero point five three eight.

142
00:04:50.499 --> 00:04:53.252
The second, v, keeps beta two, zero point

143
00:04:53.252 --> 00:04:55.775
nine nine nine, of its old value, so it

144
00:04:55.775 --> 00:04:58.242
averages the squared gradient over roughly

145
00:04:58.242 --> 00:05:00.765
the last thousand steps: this weight's

146
00:05:00.765 --> 00:05:01.913
usual gradient size.

147
00:05:02.257 --> 00:05:04.838
Here, a thousandth of zero point two eight

148
00:05:04.838 --> 00:05:05.125
nine.

149
00:05:05.615 --> 00:05:07.881
Both start at zero, so early on they're

150
00:05:07.881 --> 00:05:10.254
too small, and dividing by one minus beta

151
00:05:10.254 --> 00:05:12.735
one, or beta two, to the power t corrects

152
00:05:12.735 --> 00:05:13.004
that.

153
00:05:13.328 --> 00:05:15.647
At step one it gives back the gradient and

154
00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:16.241
its square.

155
00:05:16.564 --> 00:05:18.776
By the end of T one twenty-eight's first

156
00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:21.041
epoch the second divisor had reached zero

157
00:05:21.041 --> 00:05:22.012
point eight eight.

158
00:05:22.457 --> 00:05:24.234
The step divides the momentum by the

159
00:05:24.234 --> 00:05:26.347
square root of the scale; epsilon only

160
00:05:26.347 --> 00:05:27.596
prevents a divide by zero.

161
00:05:27.884 --> 00:05:29.997
For the toy that ratio is minus one, so w

162
00:05:29.997 --> 00:05:32.062
moves by exactly eta, to zero point five

163
00:05:32.062 --> 00:05:33.935
zero one, just as PyTorch's own Adam

164
00:05:33.935 --> 00:05:34.223
gives.

165
00:05:34.511 --> 00:05:36.192
So each weight's step is about eta

166
00:05:36.192 --> 00:05:38.161
whatever the size of its gradient, and

167
00:05:38.161 --> 00:05:40.130
shorter when its gradients keep flipping

168
00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:40.370
sign.

169
00:05:40.658 --> 00:05:42.387
Our betas and epsilon are PyTorch's

170
00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:42.819
defaults.

171
00:05:44.315 --> 00:05:46.455
Set a weight decay and the trainer uses

172
00:05:46.455 --> 00:05:48.702
AdamW instead, which also shrinks every

173
00:05:48.702 --> 00:05:51.055
weight a little each step, apart from the

174
00:05:51.055 --> 00:05:51.537
gradient.

175
00:05:51.858 --> 00:05:54.158
Apart from two tests in round two, every

176
00:05:54.158 --> 00:05:56.084
run had it at zero: plain Adam.

177
00:05:56.405 --> 00:05:58.599
Those two tests put a small decay on the

178
00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:00.846
LSTM, and the build log says it changed

179
00:06:00.846 --> 00:06:01.274
nothing.

180
00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:06.813
Before every step the trainer sets the

181
00:06:06.813 --> 00:06:08.519
learning rate from this function.

182
00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.950
With a warm-up of S steps it climbs in a

183
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.276
straight line to the full rate, then stays

184
00:06:13.276 --> 00:06:13.586
there.

185
00:06:14.015 --> 00:06:16.200
With three hundred steps, step zero runs

186
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.487
at a three-hundredth of the rate, step one

187
00:06:18.487 --> 00:06:20.519
forty-nine at half, and from step two

188
00:06:20.519 --> 00:06:22.145
ninety-nine on, the full rate.

189
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.889
The usual reason: the weights start random

190
00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:26.972
and Adam's averages have seen only a few

191
00:06:26.972 --> 00:06:29.056
gradients, so the first steps are kept

192
00:06:29.056 --> 00:06:29.360
small.

193
00:06:30.792 --> 00:06:32.743
The architecture run used three hundred

194
00:06:32.743 --> 00:06:35.134
steps, T one twenty-eight included: about

195
00:06:35.134 --> 00:06:36.500
one percent of its training.

196
00:06:36.793 --> 00:06:38.793
The genre drummers and the bass runs had

197
00:06:38.793 --> 00:06:39.037
none.

198
00:06:39.329 --> 00:06:41.525
Their plan asked for the same warm-up, but

199
00:06:41.525 --> 00:06:43.574
the night's launch settings left the flag

200
00:06:43.574 --> 00:06:43.769
out.

201
00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:45.720
Whether that mattered hasn't been

202
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.769
measured; it's a line for the report.

203
00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.473
Now the line we skipped.

204
00:06:52.781 --> 00:06:54.733
Picture two weights whose gradient is

205
00:06:54.733 --> 00:06:56.274
three and four: an arrow.

206
00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.740
Its length, the gradient norm, is the

207
00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.000
square root of the sum of the squares:

208
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:01.257
five.

209
00:07:01.565 --> 00:07:03.671
For a real model the sum runs over every

210
00:07:03.671 --> 00:07:04.442
weight at once.

211
00:07:04.865 --> 00:07:07.383
Clipping caps that length: if the gradient

212
00:07:07.383 --> 00:07:09.692
is longer than one, scale the whole arrow

213
00:07:09.692 --> 00:07:12.001
down to length one, keeping its direction.

214
00:07:12.315 --> 00:07:14.676
Three, four becomes zero point six, zero

215
00:07:14.676 --> 00:07:15.306
point eight.

216
00:07:15.621 --> 00:07:17.510
Shorter than one, it's left alone.

217
00:07:17.973 --> 00:07:20.199
Our step clips at one and keeps the length

218
00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:22.424
from before clipping; each epoch logs

219
00:07:22.424 --> 00:07:24.546
their median and the share of steps over

220
00:07:24.546 --> 00:07:24.753
one.

221
00:07:26.258 --> 00:07:28.030
Here are the real logs from the

222
00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.355
architecture run, on an axis where each

223
00:07:30.355 --> 00:07:31.850
step is ten times the last.

224
00:07:32.183 --> 00:07:34.397
The transformer's median fell from zero

225
00:07:34.397 --> 00:07:36.889
point one to zero point zero four, and the

226
00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.772
LSTM with the same reach as S one

227
00:07:38.772 --> 00:07:41.263
twenty-eight, L one twenty-eight, stayed

228
00:07:41.263 --> 00:07:43.423
between about zero point one three and

229
00:07:43.423 --> 00:07:45.914
zero point one five: far below the clip.

230
00:07:46.247 --> 00:07:48.738
The S four D model's grew instead: zero

231
00:07:48.738 --> 00:07:51.064
point two nine in the first epoch, then

232
00:07:51.064 --> 00:07:53.445
between two and a half and two point seven

233
00:07:53.445 --> 00:07:55.106
from epoch four to epoch nine.

234
00:07:55.587 --> 00:07:57.970
L one twenty-eight had one step clipped in

235
00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.187
twelve epochs, and T one twenty-eight

236
00:08:00.187 --> 00:08:02.570
none, while S one twenty-eight had three

237
00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:04.510
percent of its first epoch's steps

238
00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.727
clipped, and seventy-seven percent by

239
00:08:06.727 --> 00:08:07.337
epoch nine.

240
00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:10.053
S thirty-two reached sixty-four percent in

241
00:08:10.053 --> 00:08:10.884
its last epoch.

242
00:08:12.362 --> 00:08:14.625
So in the architecture run one setting,

243
00:08:14.625 --> 00:08:17.051
the same for every arm, only ever bit on S

244
00:08:17.051 --> 00:08:19.637
four D: the arms did not train under quite

245
00:08:19.637 --> 00:08:20.392
the same rule.

246
00:08:20.715 --> 00:08:23.033
The logged norms are how we know; the

247
00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:24.919
trainer's docstring calls them the

248
00:08:24.919 --> 00:08:26.751
evidence the fairness claim needs.

249
00:08:27.075 --> 00:08:28.799
It also calls clipping a hidden

250
00:08:28.799 --> 00:08:29.769
learning-rate cut.

251
00:08:30.093 --> 00:08:32.518
Under Adam that's too simple, because Adam

252
00:08:32.518 --> 00:08:34.781
already divides each step by the weight's

253
00:08:34.781 --> 00:08:37.206
usual gradient size; the real effect is

254
00:08:37.206 --> 00:08:39.093
subtler, and still to be checked.

255
00:08:39.416 --> 00:08:41.572
How much it changed S four D's training

256
00:08:41.572 --> 00:08:43.943
hasn't been measured: a caveat for the

257
00:08:43.943 --> 00:08:45.129
report, not a cause.

258
00:08:46.672 --> 00:08:48.687
So: the gradient is the slope of the loss

259
00:08:48.687 --> 00:08:50.403
for every weight, and each step moves

260
00:08:50.403 --> 00:08:50.874
against it.

261
00:08:51.132 --> 00:08:53.019
Adam gives each weight its own step size,

262
00:08:53.019 --> 00:08:53.919
and warm-up eases in.

263
00:08:54.176 --> 00:08:56.020
Clipping caps the gradient's length, and

264
00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:57.779
in the architecture run the log shows it

265
00:08:57.779 --> 00:08:58.679
bit only on S four D.

266
00:08:58.936 --> 00:09:00.780
This pairs with the training recipe in the

267
00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:01.509
course explainer.

268
00:09:01.767 --> 00:09:03.868
Next, lesson three point four: training a

269
00:09:03.868 --> 00:09:05.540
model that carries its state through a

270
00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:05.755
song.

